    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to test the leak",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "## In-flight leak \u2014 the rival explanation\n\nThe connector picks a Graph **app registration** by fewest in-flight requests, counted in **Redis**: `Incr()` before each call, `Decr()` after (`graphhelper.go:122-137`). Any request that dies in between leaks a count **permanently** \u2014 the 15-minute `Expire` is refreshed on every `Incr`, so it never fires under sustained load.\n\nOnce every client exceeds `maxInflight`, `getClient()` **blocks on every fetch** (`graphhelper.go:326-358`) \u2014 and that code path has **no log statement at all**, so it leaves no trace.\n\n### Reading the panel to the left\n* **sawtooth returning to 0 between scans** -> healthy\n* **monotonic ratchet that never returns to 0** -> leaking\n\n### The decisive test (2 minutes, no history needed)\n```\n# with the connector IDLE:\nKEYS  inflight:*\nGET   inflight:<clientID>    # per Graph app registration\n```\nEvery value **must** be 0 on an idle connector. Any non-zero value is a leak. Stronger: restart the pod, wait for idle, re-read \u2014 the counters live in Redis, not the pod, so a restart does not clear them.\n\n### Telling the two apart\n**Throttling** -> `\"graph DownloadDriveItem failed\"` lines with `http_status` 429.\n**Leak** -> slow fetches with **no error lines at all**."
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 28
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "row",
