    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "What this dashboard can and cannot tell you",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "## Read this before trusting a flat graph\n\n### There is no 429 counter in the `graph_*` family\n`graph_requests_5xx` counts **only** 5xx. Microsoft Graph throttling arrives as **HTTP 429**, which is 4xx, and **nothing counts it**.\n\nA flat `graph_requests_5xx` does **not** mean Graph was healthy.\n\nThrottling is visible in exactly two places:\n1. `sharepoint_content_fetch_error_total{code=\"FETCH_ERROR_RATE_LIMITED\"}` \u2014 the panels above\n2. the connector logs \u2014 grep `\"graph DownloadDriveItem failed\"`, logged at WARN with an `http_status` field\n\n### The error body is thrown away\n`internal/graphhelper/download.go:101-106` drains and **discards** the Graph error body, and never reads `Retry-After`. So we can see *that* we were throttled but not the Graph error code (`activityLimitReached`) nor how long Microsoft asked us to wait.\n\n### Why one connector's problem kills a privacy scan\ncontent-service does not enumerate `FETCH_ERROR_RATE_LIMITED` in `mapFetchError`, so it falls to the `default` branch and is treated as **retryable** \u2014 5 in-process attempts with 1/2/4/8s backoff, then Nak'd for up to 3 JetStream deliveries. **15 calls to Graph per document**, with Graph's own `Retry-After` ignored. Retrying a throttle deepens it."
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 14,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 46
      }
